summary:
Return a function pointer identical to `f`, but with its owning object changed from the object that created `f` to `ob`. This is useful when the creator of `f` has been destructed, or when `f` is an efun that needs to be called by another object.

Example:

   void make_living(object ob)
   {
       function f;

       f = bind((: enable_commands :), ob);

       evaluate(f);
   }

This has the same effect as the object `ob` calling enable_commands() itself. Because bind() can force another object to execute code, it poses security risks. To prevent misuse, the valid_bind() apply in the MASTER object must return 1; otherwise, bind() cannot execute.
